  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          The trouble with Barcelona is not scarcity. It is that the list most
          visitors arrive holding was written by a single architect, and that
          the things on it sit on four separate grounds which cannot see one
          another: a hillside in the north, a nineteenth-century grid across the
          middle, a knot of medieval lanes down by the old shore, and a hill on
          the far side of the port. In a compact city a poor decision costs ten
          minutes. Here it costs an afternoon and two metro legs, and the bill
          does not arrive until you are standing in the wrong queue at four
          o'clock with the light going.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which makes ranking close to useless. Nobody needs telling that the
          Sagrada Família is remarkable. What a stranger needs is the exchange
          rate — that a second Gaudí house interior costs the Gothic Quarter,
          that the cable car up Montjuïc costs more than the museum at the top
          of it, and that one of the finest interiors in the city is a concert
          hall nobody puts on a list at all. So the nineteen below are grouped
          into six sections by what each contributes, and each is answered on
          the same points: why it matters, how long it takes, what it sits
          beside, what needs booking, and what choosing it displaces.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two arguments run underneath the page, and it is fairer to declare them
          than to smuggle them in. The first is that Barcelona's Modernisme is
          oversold as Gaudí and badly undersold as everything else: the two
          Domènech i Montaner buildings here are cheaper, quieter and no less
          extraordinary than the third Gaudí interior anybody will sell you. The
          second is that the high ground costs less than it looks — the best view
          in the city is free and the second best is two euros. What this page does
          not do is put the nineteen in order; sequencing Barcelona is solved
          elsewhere, for <a href="/one-day-in-barcelona/">a single day</a> and for
          <a href="/7-days-in-barcelona/">a full week read through Gaudí</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

