  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="gaudi"
      number="I."
      name="Gaudí's Barcelona"
      epithet="Five entries, four of them ticketed interiors, and the case for going inside exactly one of the houses"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Gaudí left enough behind that a visitor can spend an entire stay on him
        and see nothing else. Plenty do. The problem is that the buildings are
        sold individually and consumed as a set, so a three-day trip becomes four
        entrance halls and four rooftops, and the traveller goes home having
        understood the architect and not the city. What follows is arranged to
        prevent that: two entries close to compulsory, two variations on one idea
        of which you should take one, and a fifth that almost nobody does.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="sagrada-familia" level={3} rule={false}>1. The Sagrada Família</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Begun in 1882 and still rising, and the only thing here no substitute
        approaches. The case for it is not the towers or the century of argument
        about finishing a dead man's drawings; it is the nave, built as a
        forest. The piers branch near the top into a canopy of hyperboloid
        vaults, so the loads come down through a structure borrowed from the way
        a tree stands rather than the way a Gothic cathedral does — which is why
        there are no flying buttresses on it. The glass is graded by compass
        point, warm ambers on one flank and cold greens on the other, so the
        interior changes hue across the day instead of merely brightening. Forty
        minutes gets the shape of the room; ninety gets the light doing what it
        was designed to do.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tower ascent is a step up in price and a larger one in time, and on a
        short stay it is the first thing to cut, because this city gives away better
        long views for nothing. Note too that it is a working church with a dress
        code, no general cloakroom and no tolerance for a late arrival. Overhead the
        construction is turning a corner: the central crane has finished the exterior
        of the Tower of Jesus Christ and moved on to the Glory façade.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 90 minutes inside, plus half an hour of buffer in
        front of your slot · <strong>Pairs with</strong> Sant Pau, 900 metres up
        Avinguda de Gaudí · <strong>Booking</strong> essential, timed, online
        only. Basilica with audio guide <strong>€26</strong>, guided
        <strong>€30</strong>, with a tower <strong>€36</strong>, guided and with a
        tower <strong>€40</strong>. Read
        <a href="https://sagradafamilia.org/en/tickets-individuals" rel="noopener">the
        basilica's own ticket page</a> before you commit, and its front page on the
        morning you go: liturgy, maintenance and disruption outside the walls can
        all restrict access at short notice · <strong>Skip if</strong> never. First
        visit, and the one booking worth rearranging a trip around.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="park-guell" level={3}>2. Park Güell</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A failed property development, which is the fact that makes sense of it.
        Eusebi Güell bought a bare hillside in 1900 and had Gaudí lay out sixty
        plots for a garden city above the smoke; two were built, nobody else
        came, and what survives is a public park holding the infrastructure of a
        suburb that never existed. So this is not a garden with follies in it. It
        is roads, retaining walls, a market hall and a village square, built as
        though ordinary civic apparatus deserved to be extraordinary — the
        Hypostyle Room was to be the market, and the terrace above it, edged by
        the serpentine bench, the square.
      </p>
      <p>
        It goes early in a day because it is unshaded, and early in a
        <em>trip</em> because the terrace is the best orientation lecture
        available: the Eixample runs away below in its grid, the basilica stands
        out of it, the sea closes the horizon. Spend part of the hour working out
        where everything is, then leave — the Calvary hill and the Casa Museu are
        an hour better spent elsewhere, and once you exit the restricted area the
        ticket will not let you back in.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour and a quarter in the monumental zone ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Gràcia below it and little else — the hill is
        the point and the isolation is the cost ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> yes, by time band. You have
        <strong>thirty minutes after your slot</strong> to get through the gate and
        not a minute more; the Bus Güell shuttle from Alfons X is included. Prices
        and bands are on
        <a href="https://parkguell.barcelona/en/buy-tickets" rel="noopener">the
        park's own ticket page</a> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day and
        would rather have the old city slowly. First visit otherwise.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="la-pedrera" level={3}>3. La Pedrera — the house to choose</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Casa Milà, universally called the quarry, and the right answer if you are
        going inside one Gaudí house. Its thesis is that a masonry façade can behave
        like an eroded cliff, and it holds that argument without a single straight
        line in the street front. But the reason to buy the ticket is upstairs. The
        attic is a corridor of bare parabolic brick arches, close-set and
        unornamented, doing pure structural work — the clearest statement Gaudí built
        of how his geometry carries load. Above it the roof is a landscape of helmeted
        chimneys, and the one Gaudí rooftop that gives you the Eixample from inside
        the Eixample rather than above it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The choice against Casa Batlló, three blocks south, comes down to this:
        Batlló's argument is almost entirely on its outside and La Pedrera's on its
        inside. You can read Batlló from the kerb. You cannot read this attic or
        this roof from anywhere at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 75 to 90 minutes, most of it above the second floor ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Illa de la Discòrdia three blocks down, from
        the pavement · <strong>Booking</strong> sensible in season rather than
        essential. The standard daytime visit runs from about <strong>€29</strong>
        and covers the courtyards, an apartment, the attic and the roof; a
        late-afternoon <em>Sunset</em> admission runs from about
        <strong>€25</strong> for the same route, the better buy in July and August.
        Details on
        <a href="https://www.lapedrera.com/en/visits" rel="noopener">its own visits
        page</a> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you are choosing Casa Batlló instead,
        or have one day. First visit at two days or more.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="casa-batllo" level={3}>4. Casa Batlló — and why the pavement may be enough</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The more astonishing building of the two and the weaker ticket, which is
        an unpopular thing to write and is nonetheless what the arithmetic says.
        Everything Casa Batlló is famous for faces the street: the skin of broken
        blue-green tile, the bone-like window frames, the balconies shaped like
        masks, the scaled dragon-back roof. It stands in the middle of the
        <strong>Illa de la Discòrdia</strong>, where Gaudí, Puig i Cadafalch and
        Domènech i Montaner built three houses in forty metres and argued with
        each other in tile and stone — a free fifteen-minute lesson in Modernisme
        and the best thing on Passeig de Gràcia.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inside is good and heavily produced. The light well graded from deep cobalt
        at the top to pale blue at the bottom, so every floor gets the same apparent
        brightness, is brilliant thinking; the interpretation around it has grown
        more theatrical. What you must not do is book both houses, which is a day of
        foyers on a street you could have read for free.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 75 minutes inside; 15 from the pavement, the version
        recommended on a short stay · <strong>Pairs with</strong> La Pedrera three
        blocks north and the rest of the block, at street level ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> useful rather than essential; it opens late, which
        is unusual and handy. General admission runs from about
        <strong>€29</strong> and a night visit from about <strong>€25</strong>; the
        tiers multiply, so read
        <a href="https://www.casabatllo.es/en/tickets/" rel="noopener">the house's
        own ticket page</a> rather than a reseller ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have already booked La Pedrera. The exterior,
        never.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/palau-guell-roof-spire.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/palau-guell-roof-spire-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/palau-guell-roof-spire-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/palau-guell-roof-spire-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/palau-guell-roof-spire.jpg 1365w`}
