      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Palau Güell, thirty years before La Pedrera and two minutes off La Rambla. The bat on the weathervane is an old Aragonese device, and the rubble-clad cone above the salon is a rehearsal for everything Gaudí later did with a rooftop."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="lesser-gaudi" level={3}>5. The lesser Gaudí — Palau Güell, Casa Vicens, Bellesguard</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Three houses that between them cost less than one Passeig de Gràcia
        ticket, and any of them will teach you more about how Gaudí arrived at his
        late manner than a fourth trip round a famous one.
        <strong>Palau Güell</strong>, off the lower Rambla, is the earliest and
        strangest: a town mansion of 1888 for the same patron as the park, with
        brick parabolic vaults in the basement stables, a salon rising the full
        height of the house under a perforated dome, and the roof of tile-mosaic
        chimneys above. Take it to see the architect before the world decided he
        was a genius. <strong>Casa Vicens</strong>, in Gràcia, is his first
        house — 1885, tiled in green check and marigold, closer to Mudéjar sources
        than to anything he built later, and the clearest evidence that the mature
        Gaudí was invented rather than inherited.
        <strong>Bellesguard</strong>, on the slopes of Collserola, is the one
        nobody reaches: a slate-grey tower on the site of the last medieval
        Catalan royal residence, with an attic of stepped brick arches that is the
        plainer sibling of La Pedrera's.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour each · <strong>Pairs with</strong> Palau Güell
        with the Rambla and El Raval; Casa Vicens with Gràcia and an evening there;
        Bellesguard with nothing, which is its one real cost ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none needed at any of the three; all are modestly
        priced against the famous houses · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have three
        days or fewer and have already bought a Passeig de Gràcia interior. Longer
        stay — and promoted to first visit for anyone who found the Sagrada Família's
        structure more interesting than its decoration.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>Five Gaudí entries, and the only instruction that matters: go inside one of the houses, not three.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="modernisme"
      number="II."
      name="Modernisme Without Gaudí"
      epithet="Two buildings by Lluís Domènech i Montaner, and the best value for money in Barcelona"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Modernisme was a movement, not a man, and its most complete surviving
        statements are not by Gaudí at all. Domènech i Montaner built a hospital and
        a concert hall on the same principle — that a public building should be
        lavished with tile, glass, mosaic and carved stone not as decoration but as
        an argument about what ordinary people are owed. Both are UNESCO-listed,
        both central, both cheaper than a Gaudí house, and both perhaps a third as
        crowded, which after a morning on Passeig de Gràcia is a physical relief.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="sant-pau" level={3} rule={false}>6. Recinte Modernista de Sant Pau</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A hospital designed on the theory that beauty is therapeutic. Twelve linked
        pavilions set diagonally in gardens, joined by tunnels so patients never had
        to be moved through weather, each brick and mosaic and coloured tile outside
        and vaulted, tiled and daylit within. It served as Barcelona's main hospital
        until 2009, which is the detail that changes how you look at it: these are
        not galleries dressed up, they are wards. Standing in one of the long tiled
        rooms and realising people were nursed here, under that vault, does
        something no purely aesthetic building manages.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also the most efficient half-day in the northern city, standing at the
        top of <strong>Avinguda de Gaudí</strong>, a pedestrian diagonal running just
        under a kilometre downhill straight at the Sagrada Família's Nativity façade
        with the towers growing in front of you the whole way. Do the two in that
        order and the walk is part of the visit rather than a transfer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 90 minutes to two hours, much of it outdoors ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Sagrada Família, 900 metres downhill ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none needed. Self-guided admission is
        <strong>€18</strong> before 14:00 and <strong>€17</strong> after — the rare
        case of an afternoon being cheaper; audio guide €4, guided visit €21. Open
        09:30–18:30 April to October and 09:30–17:00 November to March, last entry
        half an hour before closing, with a handful of free-entry days a year.
        Confirm on
        <a href="https://santpaubarcelona.org/en/prepara-la-teva-visita/" rel="noopener">its
        own visitor page</a> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day. First visit
        at three days or more, and the most under-rated ticket in Barcelona.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/palau-de-la-musica-skylight.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The stained-glass skylight of the Palau de la Música Catalana in Barcelona seen from below: a rectangular frame enclosing an inverted cone of amber and gold glass at its centre, ringed by radiating panes of blue, green and pink glass set with rows of women's faces, all surrounded by the dark carved and rose-patterned soffit of the ceiling."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The skylight over the concert hall — a sun of amber glass hanging down into the room, with choirs of women's faces around it. The hall was built for a choral society, and this is the ceiling they raised over the singing."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="palau-musica" level={3}>7. Palau de la Música Catalana</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The best interior in Barcelona that no list mentions, and the entry most
        likely to change a traveller's idea of the city. It was built between 1905
        and 1908 for the Orfeó Català, an amateur choral society, which is the fact
        everything follows from: not an opera house for the rich but a singing hall
        for a movement, decorated accordingly, with the history of Catalan and
        European music carried round the room in mosaic and sculpture.
      </p>
      <p>
        The hall is the only auditorium in Europe lit entirely by daylight — glass
        on all four sides and, at its centre, the inverted stained-glass sun in the
        photograph above, so a matinée needs no lamps. Behind the stage eighteen
        muses emerge half-sculpted from the wall, each with a different instrument;
        the proscenium carries Wagner's valkyries on one side and a Catalan folk
        song on the other, which tells you what this building thought it was for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour on a visit; two for a concert ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Sant Pere and the northern Born, five minutes
        away, and a short walk from the Cathedral ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> yes, a few days ahead in season. A self-guided visit
        is <strong>€20</strong> and a guided one <strong>€24</strong>; combined with
        Sant Pau it is <strong>€32</strong>, the best single purchase on this page if
        you want both. Times are on
        <a href="https://www.palaumusica.cat/en/visites/visits-and-tickets_1159168" rel="noopener">the
        Palau's own visits page</a>. If your dates allow it, go to a concert
        instead — hearing the room used is worth more than being told about it ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have a single day. First visit at two days or
        more.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="old-city"
      number="III."
      name="The Old City"
      epithet="Four entries in a square kilometre of medieval and Roman fabric, and the one that is honestly overrated"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Everything in this group sits inside the footprint of the walled town, and
        the walking between the four entries is measured in minutes rather than metro
        legs. It is the only part of Barcelona where the city does the work and you
        merely have to be there — and the part most easily lost to a badly planned
        afternoon elsewhere.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="cathedral" level={3} rule={false}>8. Barcelona Cathedral and the Barri Gòtic</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Not Gaudí's, and much older: a Gothic church begun in 1298 behind a
        neo-Gothic front finished around 1900, which is why the façade looks younger
        and fussier than everything behind it. The nave is dim and vertical, but the
        thing to find is the <strong>cloister</strong> — a square of palms and
        magnolias round a mossy fountain, ringed by chapels, with thirteen white
        geese kept in it, one for each year of the life of Santa Eulàlia, the young
        co-patron of the city who lies in the crypt. They have been there for
        centuries and are not a device for visitors; the sound of them is why the
        cloister feels like a courtyard in a town rather than an annexe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Take the quarter around it as a route rather than a wander:
        <strong>Plaça Nova</strong>, where two drum towers of the Roman wall stand
        either side of the old gate under a fragment of aqueduct;
        <strong>Carrer del Bisbe</strong> beneath its fretted bridge, a 1928
        addition and the most photographed thing here;
        <strong>Plaça de Sant Jaume</strong>, the civic centre for two thousand
        years. Better before nine or after six than at any hour between.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour for the cathedral and cloister, another for the
        lanes · <strong>Pairs with</strong> everything else in this group ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none. Cultural admission is <strong>€16</strong>,
        and the schedule is the trap: <strong>weekdays 09:30–18:30</strong> with last
        entry 17:45, <strong>Saturdays and the eves of feasts 09:30–17:15</strong>,
        and on <strong>Sundays and feast days no visitors until 14:00</strong>,
        closing at 17:00. Check
        <a href="https://catedralbcn.org/visita-turistica/horaris-visita-turistica/" rel="noopener">the
        cathedral's own hours page</a> against your dates ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> the ground is a problem; it is uneven stone
        throughout. Otherwise first visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/cathedral-cloister-geese.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A dozen white geese standing on worn stone paving in the cloister of Barcelona Cathedral, several grouped beside a mossy green fountain basin and one alone in the foreground beside a low iron railing, with a palm trunk, a Gothic arcade and an iron grille behind."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Thirteen geese, one for each year of Santa Eulàlia's life, in a cathedral cloister two minutes from the busiest street in Barcelona. This is the twenty minutes most cathedral visits miss entirely."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="placa-del-rei" level={3}>9. Plaça del Rei and the Roman city underneath</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <em>Assessed rather than entered.</em> Plaça del Rei itself — the enclosed
        courtyard of the counts of Barcelona, with the great hall on one side and
        a fan-vaulted watchtower above — is the quietest important space in the
        old city and costs nothing to stand in. Under it runs the reason this
        entry exists: some four thousand square metres of excavated Roman and
        Visigothic Barcino, laid out as a walkable underground street with a dye
        works, a laundry, a fish-salting plant and a wine cellar still in place,
        which is a far more concrete account of what a provincial Roman town did
        for a living than most museums manage. The honest note is that this page
        argues for it from the official description and from standing in the square
        above, not from having walked the excavation; practicalities are on
        <a href="https://www.barcelona.cat/museuhistoria/en" rel="noopener">the
        city history museum's own site</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 10 minutes for the square, an hour with the excavation ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the cathedral, three minutes west, and Santa Maria
        del Mar, eight east · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Ticketed but inexpensive,
        with free municipal-museum periods; closed Mondays ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> Roman archaeology leaves you cold — the square above
        is free. Longer stay.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="santa-maria-del-mar" level={3}>10. Santa Maria del Mar and El Born</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The finest building in the old city and the least like the rest of it. It was
        raised between 1329 and 1383 by the parish itself — the merchants, porters
        and shipwrights of the Ribera — and finished in fifty-four years, which for a
        church this size is nearly instantaneous. One campaign, one architect, one
        idea, and no six-hundred-year argument stapled to the front. The columns are
        plain octagons set thirteen metres apart, the widest span in medieval Gothic
        anywhere, and the effect is breadth rather than height: one unbroken room
        rather than a nave with aisles. A fire in 1936 took out the baroque furniture
        inside, and the bareness it left is now the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        The quarter behind it is the other half of the entry.
        <strong>Passeig del Born</strong> runs east to the old market hall, under
        whose floor lie the excavated streets of the district demolished to build
        the Ciutadella fortress; <strong>Carrer de Montcada</strong>, a row of
        merchant palaces, is the best-preserved medieval commercial street in the
        city and where the Picasso is.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 20 minutes in the nave, an hour more with the ticketed
        roof visit, 45 minutes for the Born · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Museu
        Picasso, two minutes north, and the waterfront, eight minutes south ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none. The ticketed cultural visit adds the crypt,
        the galleries and the roof terraces; outside those hours the nave is free,
        an arrangement the <a href="/one-day-in-barcelona/">one-day route</a> exploits
        deliberately · <strong>Skip if</strong> never. First visit.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="rambla-boqueria" level={3}>11. La Rambla and La Boqueria</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        This is the entry where a guide chooses between being useful and being
        agreeable. La Rambla is historically the most important street in
        Barcelona — a drained watercourse that became the city's promenade, the
        spine of its nineteenth-century public life, the street where the Republic
        was proclaimed and where Orwell watched the revolution turn on itself. It
        is also, at two in the afternoon in August, a poor place to spend two
        hours: 1.2 kilometres of costumed performers, souvenir kiosks and the
        densest pickpocketing in the city.
      </p>
      <p>
        So cross it rather than walk it, and cross at <strong>La Boqueria</strong>,
        which is a working market of more than two hundred traders and not a food
        court with a roof. Go in the morning, walk past the first aisles of fruit
        cups and cured-meat cones aimed at people who wandered in off the street,
        and reach the fish, the mushroom stall, the offal counters and the salt
        cod.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 20 minutes for the market, 10 to cross the street ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Palau Güell and El Raval on the west side, the
        Barri Gòtic on the east · <strong>Booking</strong> none, both free. The
        market trades <strong>Monday to Saturday, 08:00–20:30</strong> and is
        <strong>closed on Sundays and public holidays</strong> — see
        <a href="https://www.boqueria.barcelona/" rel="noopener">the market's own
        site</a>. Reach the produce stalls before about eleven ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you resent crowds: the market at noon is shoulder to
        shoulder and there is no version of it that is not. First visit for the
        market, and a firm no to the promenade as an activity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PullQuote>
      A list is only worth reading if it will tell you which three things matter
      and which four you may leave out. Otherwise it is an inventory, and the
      city already has one of those.
    </PullQuote>

    <Chapter
      id="art"
      number="IV."
      name="Art, and the Case for Choosing One"
      epithet="Three collections that explain three different Barcelonas, and no good reason to visit all of them"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        These are three of the best museums in Spain and they are not alternatives
        to each other the way two Gaudí houses are. The problem is arithmetical
        rather than aesthetic: each needs two to three hours and a clear head, they
        sit at three corners of the city, and a three-day stay that takes all of them
        has spent most of its daylight indoors. So the question is not which is best.
        It is which Barcelona you want explained.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="museu-picasso" level={3} rule={false}>12. Museu Picasso</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Not a greatest-hits collection, and people who arrive expecting one are
        reliably disappointed. What this museum holds, almost uniquely, is the
        <em>formation</em> — the work of a boy and then a young man in this city,
        from academic studies of astonishing competence made at fifteen through the
        Blue Period, arranged so that you watch a conventional prodigy decide to
        stop being one. The other reason to come is the
        <strong>Las Meninas</strong> cycle: fifty-eight canvases of 1957 in which
        Picasso dismantles Velázquez over and over, kept together in one sequence of
        rooms. It occupies five adjoining medieval merchant palaces on Carrer de
        Montcada, and the building is half the visit. Choose it if you want
        Barcelona as the place an artist came from.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> two hours · <strong>Pairs with</strong> Santa Maria del
        Mar and El Born, two minutes away — the strongest geographical pairing on this
        page · <strong>Booking</strong> sensible in season. Admission is
        <strong>€14 online</strong> against <strong>€15</strong> at the desk,
        <strong>free on the first Sunday of each month</strong> and free on Thursday
        afternoons through the winter half of the year, both requiring a reservation,
        and <strong>closed on Mondays</strong>. Detail on
        <a href="https://museupicassobcn.cat/en/plan-your-visit/buy-tickets-and-opening-hours" rel="noopener">the
        museum's own visit page</a> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you want mature
        Picasso; this is not where it is. First visit if you take one museum.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mnac-oval-hall.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The Oval Hall of the MNAC in Barcelona: tiers of white seating rising on both sides of a broad central staircase beneath a ring of tall Corinthian columns, arched windows filled with white light, and a coffered dome painted in orange, gold and pale blue, with a handful of small figures on the steps and the floor."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The Oval Hall under the dome of the Palau Nacional, built for the 1929 International Exposition. A two-euro basic ticket admits you to this room and to the roof above it, without the collection — the cheapest architectural hour in Barcelona."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="mnac" level={3}>13. MNAC — Museu Nacional d'Art de Catalunya</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The national museum of Catalan art, in the Palau Nacional built for the 1929
        Exposition, holding something no other museum on earth has: the
        <strong>Romanesque</strong> collection. In the early twentieth century, with
        remote Pyrenean churches falling down and their frescoes being sold abroad,
        Catalonia sent teams into the mountains to lift the paintings off the apse
        walls and bring them here — then rebuilt the curved apses inside the galleries
        so the frescoes sit in their true geometry rather than flat on a wall. Walking
        that sequence is like walking through twenty village churches in an hour.
        Beyond it come Gothic altarpieces, Baroque, and a strong Modernisme floor.
      </p>
      <p>
        The building explains the pricing quirk that makes this entry unusual. A
        <strong>basic ticket of €2</strong> admits you to the domed Oval Hall above
        and the rooftop viewpoint, without the collection — so the best terrace on
        Montjuïc and one of the grandest interiors in the city cost less than a
        coffee.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> three hours for the collection, 30 minutes for the
        building and roof · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the rest of Montjuïc — the
        Fundació Miró is a twelve-minute walk ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none needed. <strong>€12</strong> general, covering
        temporary shows and the rooftop and valid on two days within a month;
        <strong>€2</strong> basic as above; free to under-16s and over-65s;
        <strong>free every Saturday from 15:00</strong> and the first Sunday of the
        month; <strong>closed Mondays</strong>. Confirm on
        <a href="https://www.museunacional.cat/en/opening-hours-and-prices" rel="noopener">the
        museum's own hours and prices page</a> ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have three days or fewer and would rather have
        the old city. First visit if medieval art is your subject; otherwise longer
        stay, with the €2 building visit available at any time.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="fundacio-miro" level={3}>14. Fundació Joan Miró</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The most coherent of the three, and the one an artist designed for his own
        work. Miró gave the collection to the city and had his friend Josep Lluís
        Sert build the house for it in the 1970s: white concrete, top-lit galleries,
        terraces open to the Montjuïc pines. Because it is a single artist's whole
        arc in a building tuned to it, it asks less of you than the other two and
        gives back more per hour — and the roof terrace, with sculpture standing
        against the city below, is one of the pleasantest places to sit down in
        Barcelona. Choose it if you want Catalan modernism as a living argument
        rather than a heritage listing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 90 minutes to two hours ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> the MNAC, twelve minutes downhill, and the castle
        above · <strong>Booking</strong> none needed. <strong>€17 online</strong>
        against <strong>€18</strong> at the door, concessions about €11–€12, free
        under 12; Tuesday to Saturday 10:00–20:00 and Sunday 10:00–19:00 from April
        to October, closing at 19:00 daily in winter,
        <strong>closed Mondays</strong>. Confirm on
        <a href="https://www.fmirobcn.org/en/visit-us/opening-hours-and-prices/" rel="noopener">the
        foundation's own hours and prices page</a> ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> twentieth-century abstraction is not for you.
        Longer stay, and the best of the three if you are only on Montjuïc for
        half a day.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="hill"
      number="V."
      name="The Hill and the View"
      epithet="Two entries, and the discovery that in Barcelona the height you pay most for is not the height worth having"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Barcelona rises from the sea to the ridge of Collserola, so it has an
        unusual number of ways to get above itself — from free to thirty-nine euros
        for a view that is not four times better. This group is about that gap.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="montjuic" level={3} rule={false}>15. Montjuïc</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Not a sight but a district on a hill, and the only one of Barcelona's four
        grounds that can absorb a whole day without repeating itself. On it stand
        the two museums above, the 1929 Exposition landscape of terraces and
        fountains below the Palau Nacional, the Olympic ring of 1992, the
        botanical gardens, and at the summit the <strong>castle</strong> — an
        eighteenth-century fortress worth being blunt about: its guns pointed at
        the city more often than out to sea, and it served as a prison and a place
        of execution into the twentieth century, most notoriously for the president
        of Catalonia in 1940. Its ramparts give the best view of the working port.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical content of this entry is how you go up, because the options
        differ by a factor of six. The <strong>Funicular de Montjuïc</strong> from
        Paral·lel is part of the metro network, runs on an ordinary integrated fare
        and takes two minutes. The <strong>Telefèric de Montjuïc</strong> cable car
        onward to the castle is a separate commercial operation at
        <strong>€19 return</strong> or <strong>€12</strong> one way — more than the
        MNAC's general admission, for a ten-minute ride. It is a fine ride and it is
        not necessary: the funicular plus the 150 bus reaches the same gate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> half a day minimum; a full day with two museums ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> Plaça d'Espanya and the Exposition axis below ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none for the hill. The castle is
        <strong>free every Sunday from 15:00</strong> and all day on the first Sunday
        of the month, sells online only, and publishes hours on
        <a href="https://ajuntament.barcelona.cat/castelldemontjuic/en/visit/planning-your-visit" rel="noopener">its
        own visitor page</a>; cable-car fares are on
        <a href="https://www.telefericdemontjuic.cat/en" rel="noopener">the
        Telefèric site</a> · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day; it is a
        half day in the wrong direction. First visit at three days or more.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/tibidabo-ferris-wheel-over-the-city.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A ferris wheel with brightly coloured open cabins standing on the summit of Tibidabo above Barcelona in hazy afternoon light, the city grid running away below it to the port and the Mediterranean, a lattice mast to the right, a pine in the near foreground and a red-surfaced terrace below."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Tibidabo is a hundred-year-old fairground on the highest summit above Barcelona, and this is the honest picture of it: a ferris wheel, a haze, and the whole city between you and the sea. Whether that is worth ninety minutes each way is the argument of the next entry."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="high-ground" level={3}>16. The free high ground — and the case against Tibidabo</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <strong>Bunkers del Carmel</strong>, properly the Turó de la Rovira, is the
        best view in Barcelona and it costs nothing: a hilltop at 262 metres
        carrying the concrete emplacements of an anti-aircraft battery built in 1937
        against Italian bombers, later covered by a shanty settlement until the
        1990s and now interpreted by the city history museum. The view is a full
        circle — sea, port, the Sagrada Família standing out of the grid, Montjuïc
        opposite, Collserola behind — which no paid viewpoint manages. There is no ticket and no gate, and it is
        <strong>closed at night</strong>, a restriction imposed after the hill became
        a nightly party at the residents' expense. This entry is
        <em>assessed</em>: the argument rests on official information and geography,
        not on having stood up there.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tibidabo</strong>, by contrast, was visited, and the verdict is a
        qualified no. The summit carries three things: the
        <strong>Temple Expiatori del Sagrat Cor</strong>, a church of 1902–1961
        topped by a bronze Christ, free to enter with a small charge for the lift to
        its terraces; the oldest amusement park in Spain, opened in 1901 and still
        running its original aeroplane ride and automaton museum; and the finest
        single view in the city. The trouble is the burden — a train or bus to the
        foot of the hill, then the <em>Cuca de Llum</em> funicular, comfortably
        ninety minutes each way before you have looked at anything. A full park
        ticket is <strong>€39</strong>; a <em>Panoramic Area</em> pass covering the
        five old attractions and the transport up is <strong>€21.50</strong>. With
        children it is a good day and the park is charming rather than cynical.
        Without them, the Bunkers give a better view for nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 45 minutes at the Bunkers plus half an hour each way;
        a half day minimum for Tibidabo · <strong>Pairs with</strong> the Bunkers
        with Gràcia or Park Güell below; Tibidabo with nothing ·
        <strong>Booking</strong> none for the Bunkers or the church. Park rates are
        on
        <a href="https://tibidabo.cat/en/plan-the-visit/rates" rel="noopener">Tibidabo's
        own page</a>; the Bunkers' interpretation centre keeps short seasonal hours
        published by
        <a href="https://www.barcelona.cat/museuhistoria/en" rel="noopener">MUHBA</a>,
        though the hilltop needs neither ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> Tibidabo, unless you have children. The Bunkers:
        first visit, and go for the last hour of light.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="open-ground"
      number="VI."
      name="Water, Open Ground and the Evening"
      epithet="The unticketed half of Barcelona, which is also the half most visitors run out of time for"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Three entries, no tickets, and the part of a stay travellers most often
        discover they failed to schedule. Everything above is a room you enter and
        leave; this group is the city itself, and it is what people remember.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="waterfront" level={3} rule={false}>17. The waterfront and the beaches</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Barcelona spent most of its history with its back to the sea — the old port
        was industry, the shore was rail yards, and the beaches as they now exist
        were largely built for the 1992 Olympics, which is why the waterfront feels
        less ancient than everything behind it. Walk it rather than sit on it: from
        the <strong>Moll de la Fusta</strong> at the foot of the Rambla, past the
        working end of the old harbour and the marina of
        <strong>Port Vell</strong>, round the headland of
        <strong>Barceloneta</strong> — a triangular grid of narrow streets built in
        the 1750s for people displaced by the Ciutadella fortress, and still the
        densest neighbourhood in the city — and on up the sand towards
        <strong>Port Olímpic</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On swimming: the Barceloneta beach is the busiest and least pleasant, and
        every stop further north-east on the L4 improves it. The
        <strong>Poblenou</strong> beaches — Bogatell, Mar Bella, Nova Mar Bella — are
        wider, calmer and fifteen minutes from the old city, which is the whole trick.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> 90 minutes on foot from the Rambla to Port Olímpic ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> El Born and Santa Maria del Mar, eight minutes
        inland · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Free ·
        <strong>Skip if</strong> you have one day and no evening — the harbour is the
        right end to a single day, the beaches are not. First visit for the walk;
        longer stay for the sand.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="ciutadella" level={3}>18. Parc de la Ciutadella and the Arc de Triomf</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The one large park in the flat city, and a piece of history disguised as
        lawn. After the siege of 1714 the victorious Bourbon crown levelled a whole
        district and built a star-shaped citadel with its guns trained inward on the
        city it had just taken; Barcelona spent a century and a half trying to have
        it demolished, succeeded in the 1860s, and turned the site into a park. So
        the pleasantest green space in the old city is a deliberate erasure.
      </p>
      <p>
        In it: a monumental cascade a young Gaudí worked on as a student assistant, a
        boating lake, the parliament of Catalonia in the surviving arsenal, the zoo,
        and Domènech i Montaner's brick and iron
        <strong>Castell dels Tres Dragons</strong>, built as a café-restaurant for
        the 1888 Universal Exposition. The approach is the best part. The
        <strong>Arc de Triomf</strong> stands at the head of
        <strong>Passeig de Lluís Companys</strong>, an avenue of palms and ornate
        iron lamp standards built as the gateway to that exposition; it is red brick
        rather than stone, and its frieze welcomes the nations rather than
        commemorating a victory.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Time</strong> an hour, or two with the walk up from the arch ·
        <strong>Pairs with</strong> El Born on its west side and the Museu Picasso
        beyond · <strong>Booking</strong> none. Park and arch are free; the zoo is
        separately ticketed · <strong>Skip if</strong> you have a single day. First
        visit at three days or more, and the best place in the city to do nothing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/arc-de-triomf-from-the-avenue.jpg 2048w`}
